> Donmyar says that your bill comes to 162 zorkmids.

     You used #chat and pointed at the shopkeeper, who was
shown as 'I' (or whatever blob for "remembered unseen monster"
is used with tiles).  You were carrying at least one unpaid item
in your inventory at the time.  If all you owed was for the gold
you picked up, you would have received a different message here:
Donmyar reminds you that you owe him 29 zorkmids.

> There appears to be no shopkeeper here to receive your payment.

     You used the 'p' command, but couldn't see any shopkeeper
so no one accepted payment.  [Whether that "makes sense" after
the previous feedback is not something I want to get into. :-]

> You drop 162 gold pieces. Your debt is paid off. 133 zorkmids are added t=
o
> your credit.

     You dropped gold.  The 29 gold you owed for picking up
what the mimic dropped was paid back.  The rest of what you
dropped became store credit.  You were still carrying unpaid
shop goods totaling 133 gold (probably one 100 zorkmid item
with the randomly applied 1/3 surcharge for undiscovered items).

> 162 gold pieces. Your credit is erased. You owe Donmyar 29 zorkmids.

     You picked up all the gold you had previously dropped.
You now owe 29zm again for the mimic-dropped gold, plus 133zm
for an unpaid item in your inventory.

> You drop 29 gold pieces. Your debt is paid off.

     You dropped gold again.  This time just enough to cover
the mimic's gold.  You still owe the shopkeeper for whatever
unpaid item you're carrying, which isn't classified as shop
debt, just your current shopping bill.  You could still drop
the item(s) and owe nothing.

> You escaped the shop without paying! You stole 133 zorkmids worth of
> merchandise. An alarm sounds!

     You teleported out without paying for the unpaid item(s).
If you had re-dropped the other 133 gold first, the shopkeeper
would have given you credit again, and then used that credit
to pay for the item when you teleported out.

     Prior to teleporting, you should have used `I u' (plus
`I x' to be thorough) to see your inventory of unpaid items
and find out what you owed.  The '$' command would have worked
there too, without itemizing anything.

     The key to this whole confusing sequence is that 'p'
doesn't work when you can't see the shopkeeper.  Creating the
appropriate amount of credit before leaving is the way to cope
with that.  [Whether or not either of those things make sense,
they're intended behavior.]


